// The Ring 2 `time` module (use std.{time}): a deterministic *logical* monotonic clock (no
// wall-clock, so output is reproducible and identical on both backends). `monotonic()` reads then
// advances by one tick; `sleep(ms)` advances the clock without actually blocking.
// expect: stdout "0"
// expect: stdout "true"
// expect: stdout "7"
// expect: exit 0
use std.{time};

t0 = time.monotonic();
echo t0;

// The clock only moves forward.
t1 = time.monotonic();
echo t1 > t0;

// sleep advances the logical clock deterministically. After the two reads above the clock is at
// 2; sleep(5) takes it to 7; the next monotonic() reads 7 (then bumps to 8). The pinned value
// locks that arithmetic — proof the clock is a reproducible counter, not wall-clock.
time.sleep(5);
echo time.monotonic();
